Libre VMM Authors
=================

This file lists the primary contributors to the Libre VMM project. The
complete commit history in the Git repository at
https://github.com/LucentOpenSoftware/libre-vmm is the authoritative
record.

Original author and maintainers
-------------------------------

- The Libre VMM contributors


Direct upstream attributions
----------------------------

Libre VMM stands on the work of mature open-source projects. We thank:

- The QEMU project — the hypervisor we build on
- The libvirt project — the management API we wrap
- The TianoCore / EDK2 project — the firmware our LibreUEFI fork derives from
- The Linux kernel KVM subsystem — our acceleration substrate
- The Rust language and ecosystem teams
- The egui / eframe project — the UI toolkit
- The Axum project — the REST API framework
- The Tokio project — the async runtime
- The serde and quick-xml projects — serialization plumbing
- The spice-gtk project — the SPICE client


Projects whose ideas, patterns, or code informed Libre VMM
----------------------------------------------------------

In keeping with the project's borrow-then-rewrite policy, Libre VMM
draws inspiration from several established VM tooling projects. Where we
adopted concepts, configuration formats, or code patterns, we credit the
originating project:

- OSX-KVM (kholia/osx-kvm) — macOS guest support patterns
- vm-curator (mroboff/vm-curator) — import wizard architecture, PCI
  scanner approach, OS profile catalog
- Lima (lima-vm/lima) — automatic guest port forwarding, cloud image
  templates, file-sharing concepts
- Vectras VM (xoureldeen/Vectras-VM-Android) — QMP integration patterns,
  ROM management ideas
- Quickgui / Quickemu (quickemu-project) — OS download wizard flow,
  searchable OS catalog, SSH-detection technique
- Proxmox VE — cloud-init wiring, backup retention design, VM template
  and clone semantics, per-VM firewall rule format
- VirtualBox — snapshot tree visualization concepts, virtual media
  manager, basic / expert UI mode dichotomy
- VMware Workstation — the parity target that defines our feature
  matrix; we do not reuse any VMware code


How to be added to this file
----------------------------

Significant contributions earn an entry here. "Significant" is judged
qualitatively, not by line count: a well-targeted bug fix on a critical
path, a packaging effort for a new distribution, or a sustained
contribution to translation or documentation all qualify.

To request an entry, open a pull request adding your preferred name (or
pseudonym), optional contact, and optional brief tag of your primary
contribution area. The maintainers will review.

For one-time small contributions, the Git commit log carries authorship
and is the canonical record.
